            <li><a href="#arborist-vs-tree-service">Arborist vs. tree service: the difference that matters</a></li>
            <li><a href="#when-you-need">7 situations where you need an arborist, not just a crew</a></li>
            <li><a href="#services">Arborist services we provide in Boise</a></li>
            <li><a href="#cost">What an arborist costs in Boise</a></li>
            <li><a href="#do-you-need">Do you need an arborist to trim a tree?</a></li>
            <li><a href="#verify">How to verify an arborist's credentials</a></li>
            <li><a href="#faqs">FAQs about arborists in Boise</a></li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <h2 id="what-is-an-arborist">What Is an Arborist (and What Does "ISA-Certified" Mean)?</h2>

        <p>An arborist is a professional trained in the science of individual tree care: biology, disease and pest diagnosis, pruning technique, structural assessment, and safe work practices. Where a forester manages woodlands and a landscaper manages yards, an arborist's specialty is the health and structure of a single tree — the 60-foot silver maple over your garage, the fire-blighted pear in the front yard, the cottonwood the wind keeps testing.</p>

        <p>"ISA-certified" refers to the <a href="https://www.treesaregood.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">International Society of Arboriculture</a>, the industry's main credentialing body. ISA certification requires documented experience, a rigorous exam covering tree biology through safety, and continuing education to keep the credential current. It's the standard our work is held to on every job.</p>

        <p>Why it matters: trees hide their injuries. A bad pruning cut doesn't look bad the day it's made — it looks bad in year three, when decay has moved into the trunk through a wound the tree couldn't close. Certification is the difference between someone who knows where a cut heals and someone who knows how to run a saw.</p>

        <h2 id="arborist-vs-tree-service">Arborist vs. Tree Service: The Difference That Matters</h2>

        <p>Every arborist does tree work. Not everyone doing tree work is an arborist. A "tree service" is any company with a truck, a chipper, and a business card — some are excellent, and some are why our <a href="/blog/tree-service-storm-chaser-scams-boise/">storm-chaser scams guide</a> exists.</p>

        <p><strong>The practical test:</strong> a tree service tells you what they can cut. An arborist tells you what the tree needs — which is sometimes nothing, sometimes a $200 thinning instead of a $2,000 removal, and sometimes "this one's dangerous, don't wait." The diagnosis comes before the chainsaw, not after.</p>

        <p>For routine clearance work on a healthy tree, a competent insured crew is fine. For anything involving a tree's health, structure, or safety, you want a certified arborist making the call. Our guide on <a href="/blog/how-to-hire-tree-service-boise/">how to hire a tree service in Boise</a> covers the full vetting checklist.</p>

        <h2 id="when-you-need">7 Situations Where You Need an Arborist</h2>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>The tree looks sick and you don't know why.</strong> Yellowing leaves in summer, thinning canopy, <a href="/blog/sap-dripping-from-tree/">sap dripping</a>, fungus at the base. Diagnosis before treatment — a <a href="/blog/tree-health-assessment-boise/">tree health assessment</a> identifies the problem instead of guessing at it.</li>
          <li><strong>A tree is leaning, cracked, or hollow.</strong> Whether a <a href="/blog/leaning-tree-dangerous-boise/">leaning tree</a> or <a href="/blog/hollow-tree-safe-or-hazard/">hollow trunk</a> is a hazard or harmless is exactly the judgment call arborists are trained for. Guessing wrong in either direction is expensive.</li>
          <li><strong>The tree is near your house, or over anything valuable.</strong> Pruning or removal within falling distance of structures requires rigging decisions that come from structural assessment, not intuition.</li>
          <li><strong>You're pruning a mature tree.</strong> A mature shade tree can't regrow its structure. Every large cut is permanent. Proper <a href="/blog/crown-thinning-raising-reduction/">crown thinning, raising, or reduction</a> keeps the tree sound; topping ruins it for good.</li>
          <li><strong>A valuable tree needs saving, not removing.</strong> Cabling and bracing, soil treatment, and corrective pruning can rescue trees a removal-only crew would fell by default.</li>
          <li><strong>You need documentation.</strong> Insurance claims, real estate deals, and neighbor disputes often require a <a href="/blog/arborist-report-when-you-need-one-boise/">written arborist report</a> — a formal assessment from a credentialed professional.</li>
          <li><strong>Storm damage left a tree half-standing.</strong> Partially failed trees are the most dangerous work in this industry. That's <a href="/emergency-tree-service-boise/">emergency tree service</a> territory: call before anyone improvises with a ladder.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2 id="services">Arborist Services We Provide in Boise</h2>

        <h3>Tree Health Assessments</h3>
        <p>An arborist inspects the tree from root flare to canopy: soil condition, trunk wounds, decay indicators, pest and disease signs, and structural defects. You get a straight answer about what's wrong and what (if anything) to do about it. Runs $75&ndash;$250, often waived when you schedule the recommended work. Full details on our <a href="/blog/tree-health-assessment-boise/">tree health assessment page</a>.</p>

        <h3>Tree Risk Assessments</h3>
        <p>Focused on one question: how likely is this tree (or limb) to fail, and what would it hit? Essential for trees near houses, play areas, and driveways, and for <a href="/blog/buying-home-mature-trees-boise/">buying a home with mature trees</a>. After Boise's spring windstorms, this is our most-requested consultation.</p>

        <h3>Fine and Structural Pruning</h3>
        <p>Pruning to ANSI A300 standards: crown thinning for wind resistance, structural pruning to build sound scaffolds in young trees, clearance raising, deadwood removal. The full timing-and-technique breakdown is on our <a href="/tree-trimming-boise/">tree trimming page</a>.</p>

        <h3>Cabling and Bracing</h3>
        <p>Support systems for trees with weak unions or heavy limbs worth saving — a steel or synthetic cable system runs $200&ndash;$900 installed. See our <a href="/blog/tree-cabling-bracing-boise/">cabling and bracing guide</a> for when it works and when it's postponing the inevitable.</p>

        <h3>Pest and Disease Diagnosis</h3>
        <p>Fire blight in ornamental pears, borers in birches, <a href="/blog/pine-tree-problems-boise/">pine problems</a>, and the rest of the Treasure Valley's greatest hits. Identification first, then a treatment plan matched to the actual problem — see the <a href="/blog/tree-diseases-boise/">common Boise tree diseases guide</a>.</p>

        <h3>Consultations and Second Opinions</h3>
        <p>Another company says the tree has to come down? A consulting visit is cheap insurance before a four-figure removal. We'll tell you if we agree — and we'll tell you if we don't.</p>

        <h2 id="cost">What Does an Arborist Cost in Boise?</h2>

        <p>Two different things get called "arborist cost": the assessment (paying for expertise) and the work (paying for the job). Here's how both price out in the Boise area in 2026:</p>

        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Service</th><th>Typical Cost</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Estimate for specific work</td><td>Free</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Tree health assessment</td><td>$75&ndash;$250 (often waived if work is scheduled)</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Deadwood removal</td><td>$150&ndash;$800</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Cabling and bracing</td><td>$200&ndash;$900 per system</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Crown thinning</td><td>$250&ndash;$1,500</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Tree trimming (full range)</td><td>$150&ndash;$2,000+</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Tree removal (full range)</td><td>$300&ndash;$5,000+</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <p>These are ballpark ranges, not quotes — every tree and site is different. For the complete pricing breakdown by service, see <a href="/blog/arborist-cost-boise/">how much an arborist costs in Boise</a>. If you need a formal written report for insurance or a real estate transaction, that's priced separately — here's <a href="/blog/arborist-report-when-you-need-one-boise/">when you need an arborist report</a>.</p>

        <h2 id="do-you-need">Do You Need an Arborist to Trim a Tree?</h2>

        <p>Honest answer: legally, no. Idaho doesn't license tree trimming, and nothing stops you from pruning your own trees.</p>

        <p><strong>When DIY is reasonable:</strong> small ornamentals and fruit trees under about 15 feet, cuts you can make with both feet on the ground, branches under 2 inches thick, and nothing near a structure or line. Learn where to cut and the tree will be fine.</p>

        <p><strong>When it isn't:</strong> mature shade trees, anything requiring a ladder plus a saw (the classic amateur-injury combination), limbs over roofs or fences, anything within reach of a power line (that's <a href="/blog/tree-near-power-lines-boise/">Idaho Power's territory or a pro's</a> — never yours), and any structural decision on a tree you care about. Mature trees don't get do-overs: a topping job or a set of flush cuts is permanent damage, and fixing bad pruning costs far more than good pruning would have.</p>

        <p>The middle path many Boise homeowners take: pay for an assessment, have the arborist mark and explain what should come off, and do the small ground-level cuts yourself while a crew handles the canopy.</p>

        <h2 id="verify">How to Verify an Arborist's Credentials (Ours Included)</h2>

        <p>Anyone can print "certified arborist" on a truck door. Verification takes two minutes:</p>

        <ul>
          <li><strong>Check ISA certification</strong> at <a href="https://www.treesaregood.org/findanarborist" target="_blank" rel="noopener">treesaregood.org</a> — the ISA's free lookup verifies any individual's credential by name.</li>
          <li><strong>Ask for insurance certificates</strong> — general liability AND workers' compensation, issued by the insurer, not a photocopy. If a climber gets hurt on your property and the company isn't covered, that can become your problem.</li>
          <li><strong>Ask how they'd do the job.</strong> An arborist talks about which cuts and why. If the answer to every tree is "top it" or "take it down," you're talking to a chainsaw, not an arborist.</li>
        </ul>

        <p>We hold every job to ISA standards and ANSI A300 pruning specifications, and we're licensed and insured. Ask us for documentation when we quote your job — a company that hesitates on that question has answered it.</p>

        <h2 id="faqs">FAQs About Arborists in Boise</h2>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <h3>How much does an arborist cost in Boise?</h3>
          <p>A tree health assessment runs $75&ndash;$250, often waived if you schedule work afterward. Estimates for specific jobs are free. The work itself ranges from $150 for small pruning jobs to $5,000+ for large removals. Formal written reports for insurance or legal purposes are priced separately.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <h3>Do you need an arborist to trim a tree?</h3>
          <p>Legally, no — Idaho doesn't require a license for tree trimming. Practically: small trees with ground-level cuts are fine to DIY, but mature trees, anything near structures or power lines, and any cut requiring a ladder belong with a professional. Bad pruning damage is permanent and shows up years later.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <h3>What is the difference between an arborist and a tree service?</h3>
          <p>An arborist is a certified tree care professional (ISA-certified) trained in tree biology, diagnosis, and care. A tree service is a company that does tree work, which may or may not employ certified arborists. For health assessments, diagnosis, and pruning decisions, you want a certified arborist involved.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="faq-item">
          <h3>Are arborist consultations free?</h3>
          <p>Estimates for specific work are free. A diagnostic health assessment — inspecting a sick or hazardous tree and explaining what's wrong — runs $75&ndash;$250 and is often credited back if you schedule the recommended work.</p>
        </div>

